WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.834 --> 00:00:02.035 align:start size:94% position:6%
-In a time when --

00:00:02.035 --> 00:00:04.337 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it´s still an uneasy time
for Obamacare.

00:00:04.337 --> 00:00:06.172 align:start size:94% position:6%
But at a time when Obamacare
was under attack,

00:00:06.172 --> 00:00:07.574 size:88% position:44%
you were pushing forward

00:00:07.574 --> 00:00:09.609 align:start size:94% position:6%
with your
"Medicare for all" proposal.

00:00:09.609 --> 00:00:11.277 align:start size:94% position:6%
Some centrist Democrats
were even saying,

00:00:11.277 --> 00:00:12.712 size:78% position:39%
"Now is not the time.

00:00:12.712 --> 00:00:14.981 align:start size:94% position:6%
You´re going to
scare Republicans

00:00:14.981 --> 00:00:17.083
into repealing Obamacare,"
´cause they´re so worried

00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:19.452
about what you were doing
with Medicare for all.

00:00:19.452 --> 00:00:21.521
Make the argument for why you
can do both at the same time --

00:00:21.521 --> 00:00:23.656 align:start size:94% position:6%
protect Obamacare
and push for something else.

00:00:23.656 --> 00:00:27.527
-There was not many people
in the United States Congress

00:00:27.527 --> 00:00:29.529
who worked harder than I did.

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:31.498
I went all over this country,

00:00:31.498 --> 00:00:33.533 align:start size:94% position:6%
held rallies
all over this country

00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:35.301 align:start size:94% position:6%
to make certain
that the Republicans

00:00:35.301 --> 00:00:37.771
would not get away with throwing
up to 30 million people

00:00:37.771 --> 00:00:39.539 align:start size:94% position:6%
off of the health insurance
they have.

00:00:39.539 --> 00:00:41.074 size:78% position:39%
Can you imagine that?

00:00:41.074 --> 00:00:42.809 align:start size:94% position:6%
People with cancer,
with heart disease,

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:45.512
with diabetes,
with life-threatening illnesses,

00:00:45.512 --> 00:00:47.814 align:start size:94% position:6%
and these guys wanted to throw
up the 30 million people

00:00:47.814 --> 00:00:49.549 align:start size:94% position:6%
off the health insurance
they had,

00:00:49.549 --> 00:00:51.684
and no doubt
many thousands of people a year

00:00:51.684 --> 00:00:53.286
would have died as a result.

00:00:53.286 --> 00:00:55.889
Fortunately, thank God, we
were able to beat that back.

00:00:55.889 --> 00:00:59.092
So I did everything that I could

00:00:59.092 --> 00:01:01.528 align:start size:94% position:6%
to protect
the Affordable Care Act.

00:01:01.528 --> 00:01:04.497 align:start size:94% position:6%
But what we also,
all of us, understand

00:01:04.497 --> 00:01:06.800 align:start size:94% position:6%
is the Affordable Care Act
is not good enough.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:08.435 size:84% position:42%
We have got to do more.

00:01:08.435 --> 00:01:10.670 align:start size:94% position:6%
You have 28 million people
in this country today

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:12.005
who have no health insurance.

00:01:12.005 --> 00:01:13.840 align:start size:94% position:6%
You have even more
who are underinsured

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:15.875 align:start size:94% position:6%
with high deductibles,
high co-payments.

00:01:15.875 --> 00:01:18.378
We pay the highest prices in the
world for prescription drugs.

00:01:18.378 --> 00:01:20.113 align:start size:94% position:6%
One out of five people
can´t even afford

00:01:20.113 --> 00:01:22.182 align:start size:94% position:6%
the medicine
their doctors prescribe.

00:01:22.182 --> 00:01:23.450 size:84% position:42%
We have got to do more.

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:25.685
We have got to do what
every other country does,

00:01:25.685 --> 00:01:28.388 align:start size:94% position:6%
and that is get
private insurance companies

00:01:28.388 --> 00:01:30.790 size:88% position:44%
out of health insurance,

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:33.359 align:start size:94% position:6%
guarantee healthcare
to all people.

00:01:33.359 --> 00:01:35.161 size:84% position:42%
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:35.161 --> 00:01:38.064 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the best way
that we can do it --

00:01:38.064 --> 00:01:40.867 size:75% position:38%
We have a successful
healthcare program.

00:01:40.867 --> 00:01:42.068
You know what the most popular

00:01:42.068 --> 00:01:44.370 align:start size:94% position:6%
health-insurance program
in America is now?

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.172 align:start size:94% position:6%
Take a guess.
-Uh, Medicare.

00:01:46.172 --> 00:01:48.475 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Smart guy.
-Uh-huh.

00:01:48.475 --> 00:01:50.076 size:81% position:41%
-It is. It´s Medicare.

00:01:50.076 --> 00:01:52.145
So if Medicare is working,

00:01:52.145 --> 00:01:53.680 align:start size:94% position:6%
if seniors feel good
about Medicare,

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:57.016 align:start size:94% position:6%
why don´t we expand Medicare
to all of our people?

00:01:57.016 --> 00:01:58.284
And that is the legislation

00:01:58.284 --> 00:02:00.720 size:88% position:44%
that I have introduced
over a four-year period.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:03.123 align:start size:94% position:6%
We would make sure that
every person in this country

00:02:03.123 --> 00:02:04.557
had healthcare as a right.

00:02:04.557 --> 00:02:06.759
We would save middle-class
and working-class people

00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:08.795 align:start size:94% position:6%
substantial sums of money
by doing that.

00:02:08.795 --> 00:02:10.763 size:78% position:39%
-Fantastic. So you --

00:02:10.763 --> 00:02:12.699 size:84% position:42%
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:12.699 --> 00:02:14.134
In your traveling around,

00:02:14.134 --> 00:02:17.770
many people consider you
a voice of the working man.

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:19.506
You were -- Back in the day,

00:02:19.506 --> 00:02:22.308 align:start size:94% position:6%
before you got this cushy
Senate job, you --

00:02:22.308 --> 00:02:23.676 align:start size:94% position:6%
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:23.676 --> 00:02:26.513
You were -- You had a carpentry
company here in New York?

00:02:26.513 --> 00:02:29.816 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I wouldn´t go that far.
-Okay.

00:02:29.816 --> 00:02:32.085 align:start size:94% position:6%
Creative Carpenters.
Is that what it was called?

00:02:32.085 --> 00:02:34.087 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, we were creative
with the title.

00:02:34.087 --> 00:02:35.421 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Okay.

00:02:35.421 --> 00:02:36.990 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:39.926
I will say, I don´t know if
I want my carpentry creative.

00:02:39.926 --> 00:02:41.928 align:start size:94% position:6%
Yeah.
-I was able to bang nails.

00:02:41.928 --> 00:02:44.664
And I did that when I was up --
It was a lot of fun.

00:02:44.664 --> 00:02:47.000
I worked for a small contractor
guy who built homes.

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:48.601 align:start size:94% position:6%
It was a lot of fun
banging nails

00:02:48.601 --> 00:02:50.470 size:75% position:38%
and putting up roofs
and all that stuff.

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:53.273 align:start size:94% position:6%
-But was that about the height
of your carpentry skills?

00:02:53.273 --> 00:02:55.275 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You will not find
my creative carpentry

00:02:55.275 --> 00:02:57.410 align:start size:94% position:6%
in your local
fine furniture store.

00:02:57.410 --> 00:02:59.012
That I can guarantee you.

00:02:59.012 --> 00:03:00.647 align:start size:94% position:6%
-"That´s a chair
Bernie Sanders made.

00:03:00.647 --> 00:03:01.981 align:start size:94% position:6%
Don´t sit in it!"

00:03:01.981 --> 00:03:05.118 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
-Don´t laugh.

00:03:05.118 --> 00:03:07.353 size:88% position:44%
-Also, I´m happy to hear

00:03:07.353 --> 00:03:11.324
that you have been a frugal
person for your entire life.

00:03:11.324 --> 00:03:12.759 align:start size:94% position:6%
Is that safe to say?
-Yeah.

00:03:12.759 --> 00:03:15.395
-And some old friends of yours

00:03:15.395 --> 00:03:18.298 align:start size:94% position:6%
were talking about,
back in the day in Vermont,

00:03:18.298 --> 00:03:19.999 align:start size:94% position:6%
that you had --

00:03:19.999 --> 00:03:21.968 align:start size:94% position:6%
you would cook over something
that they called Berno.

00:03:21.968 --> 00:03:23.870
Will you explain what Berno is?

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:25.505
-Well, it´s a --
I don´t know what even existed.

00:03:25.505 --> 00:03:26.839 size:88% position:44%
You know what Sterno is?

00:03:26.839 --> 00:03:29.909
-Sterno. Yeah. It´s sort of like
camping food basically.

00:03:29.909 --> 00:03:32.879 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, so this was a variation
of that using toilet paper,

00:03:32.879 --> 00:03:34.681 align:start size:94% position:6%
I might say.

00:03:34.681 --> 00:03:37.450
Pouring some stuff on it,
we would cook on that.

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:38.685 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So you --

00:03:38.685 --> 00:03:40.486 align:start size:94% position:6%
-We have come a long way
since those days.

00:03:40.486 --> 00:03:42.622
Why are you taking back --

00:03:42.622 --> 00:03:44.023 size:88% position:44%
-I can´t believe the guy

00:03:44.023 --> 00:03:46.659 align:start size:94% position:6%
who figured out
how to cook over toilet paper

00:03:46.659 --> 00:03:48.962 align:start size:94% position:6%
didn´t make
all that Facebook money.

00:03:48.962 --> 00:03:52.165 align:start size:94% position:6%
Thank you so much. It´s always
a pleasures to see you.

00:03:52.165 --> 00:03:55.034 align:start size:94% position:6%
Continued good work.
And please come back soon.

00:03:55.034 --> 00:03:56.536 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Thanks very much.

